{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792853855,"nonce":"FPkAeI8/cpvNTP0sT3aUCiFSUVo=","title":"TypeScript: static class fields, static blocks and static this","message":"95 official rows stopped at 'static fields' and 16 at static blocks. A static field becomes a module global that the class declaration initializes in the module body, in class body order with static blocks; reads from function bodies check the class's ready flag (ReferenceError in its temporal dead zone) and fields declared without a value trap until assigned. In static methods, initializers and blocks, this.x and super.x name the class's and the base's statics, and this.m() calls static methods. Checked as TypeScript does: use before the class declaration at module level (2449), use of a field before its initialization in static code (2729), private and protected statics (2341, 2445), readonly statics (2540), duplicates (2300) and incompatible static sides (2417). Unsupported: reference-typed statics, string statics assigned after declaration, assignments to inherited statics through a subclass, static this through subclasses that redeclare the member, class objects as values. The same change lets an unannotated declaration that nothing references keep TypeScript's evolving type, since TypeScript never needs a type for it.","files":null}]}